00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:28.430
And again, my Excel
experience is nuanced.

00:10:28.430 --> 00:10:33.650
I would say, let's
say 90% of people

00:10:33.650 --> 00:10:35.990
using Excel are using
it as a big calculator.

00:10:35.990 --> 00:10:39.260
Everything's sort of ad
hoc, just thrown together.

00:10:39.260 --> 00:10:41.060
Maybe they're
using pivot tables.

00:10:41.060 --> 00:10:47.010
But there's no real modeling
workflow to their Excel work.

00:10:47.010 --> 00:10:49.490
Then you have maybe 10% of
people that are actually

00:10:49.490 --> 00:10:53.480
using Excel in a more advanced
way, where they actually

00:10:53.480 --> 00:10:57.500
have an inputs and
assumptions tab, which is just

00:10:57.500 --> 00:11:00.830
straight-up text explaining
the purpose of the model

00:11:00.830 --> 00:11:05.120
and what's going to happen
in the subsequent tabs.

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:07.370
And you go from your records--

00:11:07.370 --> 00:11:12.808
maybe one tab is just
straight tabular data, names

00:11:12.808 --> 00:11:14.600
or identifiers on the
left and then a bunch

00:11:14.600 --> 00:11:16.225
of different calculations
on the right.

00:11:16.225 --> 00:11:19.550
And then going left to
right or right to left,

00:11:19.550 --> 00:11:22.730
you get to summaries that are
actually consumable for leaders

00:11:22.730 --> 00:11:24.290
in the business.

00:11:24.290 --> 00:11:28.910
And then there's probably
0.1% of Excel users

00:11:28.910 --> 00:11:33.460
that are using it in
a pretty advanced way,

00:11:33.460 --> 00:11:38.070
whether it's logistic
regressions or different types

00:11:38.070 --> 00:11:40.830
of linear regressions.

00:11:40.830 --> 00:11:43.840
You could do a lot of
Monte Carlo simulations.

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:46.050
There's a lot you can do.

00:11:46.050 --> 00:11:49.560
When you start feeling
like the GUI itself isn't

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:54.390
sufficient enough,
VBA, or Visual Basic,

00:11:54.390 --> 00:12:00.060
it's a programming language that
plays hand in hand with Excel.

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:03.660
And you can actually do
a lot with automating

00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:07.470
the workflow, or the sequence
of calculations or group buys

00:12:07.470 --> 00:12:11.160
or whatever, with VBA in Excel.

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:14.640
I will say, that is not
typical of Excel users.

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:16.680
It's also very limiting.

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:23.430
Because with more open-source
programming languages,

00:12:23.430 --> 00:12:26.670
not only with the
languages themselves

00:12:26.670 --> 00:12:28.650
but with all the
libraries that are

00:12:28.650 --> 00:12:33.030
available for these
languages, there's

00:12:33.030 --> 00:12:35.950
just so much development
happening on a daily basis.

00:12:35.950 --> 00:12:43.560
And I feel like for what you
described, while it could

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:48.930
potentially be done in VBA if
you wanted to scale something

00:12:48.930 --> 00:12:51.780
that is consumable for
that business organization

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:54.570
or for other data
analysts or business

00:12:54.570 --> 00:12:58.110
analysts at your
company, you probably

00:12:58.110 --> 00:13:00.150
want to take the results
of those findings

00:13:00.150 --> 00:13:03.340
and push them into some data
warehouse or something that,

00:13:03.340 --> 00:13:06.550
again, is consumable to
a larger organization.

00:13:06.550 --> 00:13:08.850
And in order to
do that, you need

00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:12.090
to figure out, OK, well where
is this code going to live?

00:13:12.090 --> 00:13:14.220
When is it going
to be refreshed?

00:13:14.220 --> 00:13:16.170
Is it going to be retrained?

00:13:16.170 --> 00:13:18.990
How is it going to be retrained?

00:13:18.990 --> 00:13:23.280
What systems are going to help
us take this overall logic

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:26.760
and put it into a production
setting so that me, as a data

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:29.070
analyst or a business
leader, can just

00:13:29.070 --> 00:13:31.200
access some table
on a data warehouse

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:34.200
and see the results I
want to see very quickly

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:38.400
and know that they're
updated at any point in time?

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:44.050
That's where I feel like
moving towards Python or R

00:13:44.050 --> 00:13:46.390
opens your world
of possibilities

00:13:46.390 --> 00:13:50.410
within the context of
automation and machine learning.

00:13:50.410 --> 00:13:51.290
I don't know.

00:13:51.290 --> 00:13:52.832
Bill, does that
answer your question?

