00:14:42.683 --> 00:14:43.350
Yeah, that's--

00:14:43.350 --> 00:14:46.950
BILL MARTIN: I find
myself rambling.

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:51.030
And I think the main thing,
I just wanted to know,

00:14:51.030 --> 00:14:52.830
so you come back
to this new place.

00:14:52.830 --> 00:14:55.290
Do you have the
tools to do this?

00:14:55.290 --> 00:14:56.820
And are you making an impact?

00:14:56.820 --> 00:14:59.490
Or are other people still
on their own computers

00:14:59.490 --> 00:15:03.720
doing Excel and
being happy and being

00:15:03.720 --> 00:15:06.780
able to present a report
every few weeks to somebody

00:15:06.780 --> 00:15:09.270
who pats them on the back
and says, hey, great?

00:15:09.270 --> 00:15:09.780
OK.

00:15:09.780 --> 00:15:14.400
So there's 70-plus
thousand people at Centene.

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:15.435
It's a massive company.

00:15:19.830 --> 00:15:22.410
I think they're like,
a Fortune 26 company.

00:15:22.410 --> 00:15:25.170
You really don't know the
name because, by design,

00:15:25.170 --> 00:15:29.250
compared to the other
health insurance carriers,

00:15:29.250 --> 00:15:31.960
they wanted to have kind of a
portfolio management company

00:15:31.960 --> 00:15:38.550
and then each state that we
operate in has their own brand.

00:15:38.550 --> 00:15:42.360
With that, the majority
of our analysts,

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:45.360
and probably data scientists
and biostatisticians,

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:53.070
work for the brands doing
plan-specific analyzes.

00:15:53.070 --> 00:15:57.180
Where I'm at now is kind
of the corporate group.

00:15:57.180 --> 00:16:03.920
And we partner with
stakeholders in the business

00:16:03.920 --> 00:16:12.510
at different operating units or
we partner with idea generators

00:16:12.510 --> 00:16:17.150
that want, that
have a business use

00:16:17.150 --> 00:16:21.900
case that can be scaled for
the entirety of our business,

00:16:21.900 --> 00:16:24.440
not just one plan.

00:16:24.440 --> 00:16:28.430
From that perspective,
I think there

00:16:28.430 --> 00:16:30.830
are some differences between--

00:16:30.830 --> 00:16:32.960
I roll up into IT now.

00:16:32.960 --> 00:16:35.930
And I definitely
sympathize with you.

00:16:35.930 --> 00:16:37.760
Because when I was
previously at Centene,

00:16:37.760 --> 00:16:39.740
I was actually working
for Health Net, which

00:16:39.740 --> 00:16:41.580
is one of the state plans.

00:16:41.580 --> 00:16:46.400
And there's a lot that I wanted
to do to put into production,

00:16:46.400 --> 00:16:50.630
but there's so much
sensitive data in production

00:16:50.630 --> 00:16:59.280
that corporate sort of helps
manage the whole process

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:02.640
of getting a model into
an actual production

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:06.430
setting, a model or a Shiny
app or something like this.

00:17:06.430 --> 00:17:10.260
So you can do a lot as a
data scientist in a business

00:17:10.260 --> 00:17:13.608
org in the development
and test environments

00:17:13.608 --> 00:17:14.400
that are out there.

00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:17.369
But when it gets to
production, then there

00:17:17.369 --> 00:17:23.880
is somewhat of a handoff
in ownership, if you will,

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:29.220
or responsibility of getting
that code into a production

00:17:29.220 --> 00:17:30.910
setting.

00:17:30.910 --> 00:17:33.870
And that's where
there's a handshake

00:17:33.870 --> 00:17:36.810
or pass off between
the business scientists

00:17:36.810 --> 00:17:42.220
and then the corporate
data science team.

00:17:42.220 --> 00:17:45.280
And there's 70,000 people.

00:17:45.280 --> 00:17:48.310
We have an ongoing
chat of about 500

00:17:48.310 --> 00:17:50.110
data analysts and
data scientists.

00:17:50.110 --> 00:17:56.500
There's different, again,
clinical informatics people

00:17:56.500 --> 00:17:57.790
and biostatisticians.

00:17:57.790 --> 00:18:02.620
And there's a lot of very
interesting, niche work

00:18:02.620 --> 00:18:04.310
going on.

00:18:04.310 --> 00:18:08.560
But the one thing that I really
love about this organization

00:18:08.560 --> 00:18:12.230
is the knowledge sharing.

00:18:12.230 --> 00:18:14.500
There's a lot of that
that happens organically.

00:18:17.170 --> 00:18:21.200
I love that there's not a lot
of shame in asking questions.

00:18:21.200 --> 00:18:22.100
It's encouraged.

00:18:22.100 --> 00:18:26.240
If you don't know something,
go ahead and ask it.

00:18:26.240 --> 00:18:30.230
I have no shame when it comes
to asking people for help.

00:18:30.230 --> 00:18:34.910
But at the same time, on
the flip side of this,

00:18:34.910 --> 00:18:36.545
if someone comes to
me, I'm definitely

00:18:36.545 --> 00:18:38.170
going to try and make
some time to help

00:18:38.170 --> 00:18:41.770
this individual
with their problems

00:18:41.770 --> 00:18:44.750
just to kind of
balance it all out.

00:18:44.750 --> 00:18:46.230
Absolutely.

00:18:46.230 --> 00:18:49.760
And I always love seeing in
the chat everybody commenting

00:18:49.760 --> 00:18:52.970
in on what they're experiencing
in their organizations too.

00:18:52.970 --> 00:18:56.240
And, Ness, I love to hear
what you're doing there too.

00:18:56.240 --> 00:18:57.920
That's great.

00:18:57.920 --> 00:19:00.830
I see, Eric, you had
asked a question a bit

00:19:00.830 --> 00:19:01.980
earlier in the chat.

00:19:01.980 --> 00:19:03.110
Do you want to jump in?

00:19:03.110 --> 00:19:03.860
Yeah.

00:19:03.860 --> 00:19:07.580
It's great to have you,
Javier on the hangout.

00:19:07.580 --> 00:19:11.420
As part of my organization
and the group I'm in,

00:19:11.420 --> 00:19:12.980
I would say we're
kind of growing

00:19:12.980 --> 00:19:18.110
into getting AI and ML more
entrenched in some of our work.

00:19:18.110 --> 00:19:22.160
But leadership will hear all the
positive buzz from various tech

00:19:22.160 --> 00:19:25.490
sectors and they say,
we want to use ML.

00:19:25.490 --> 00:19:28.310
But what I want
your perspective on,

00:19:28.310 --> 00:19:29.930
if you could share
any insights, is,

00:19:29.930 --> 00:19:35.420
how do you approach finding best
use cases to use the algorithms

00:19:35.420 --> 00:19:39.350
without going down potentially
dead ends or making sure

00:19:39.350 --> 00:19:42.710
that leadership
understands that ML is not

00:19:42.710 --> 00:19:46.160
going to magically be the
perfect solution for every use

00:19:46.160 --> 00:19:47.215
case?

00:19:47.215 --> 00:19:48.590
I don't know if
that makes sense.

00:19:48.590 --> 00:19:50.610
Yeah, for sure.

00:19:50.610 --> 00:19:53.570
I would say-- and I'm
new to this process.

00:19:53.570 --> 00:19:56.990
Because, again, I was a
one-off data scientist

00:19:56.990 --> 00:20:00.965
in a business org prior to
joining this corporate team.

00:20:03.560 --> 00:20:06.500
And so I'm still learning about
all our systems and everything

00:20:06.500 --> 00:20:07.430
that's available.

00:20:07.430 --> 00:20:12.390
But one aspect of
this team's work,

00:20:12.390 --> 00:20:17.450
which I think could be
applicable at other companies,

00:20:17.450 --> 00:20:22.520
is to have a really
structured intake process.

00:20:22.520 --> 00:20:26.120
For people that have new--

00:20:26.120 --> 00:20:28.400
everyone's excited
about ML and AI.

00:20:28.400 --> 00:20:37.040
But there should be some sort
of detailed or an educated

00:20:37.040 --> 00:20:40.775
guesstimate of ROI
for the projects

00:20:40.775 --> 00:20:41.900
that people want to put in.

00:20:41.900 --> 00:20:47.720
And that's really hard to do,
even for experienced developers

00:20:47.720 --> 00:20:48.920
and data scientists.

00:20:48.920 --> 00:20:54.900
So I would say if
you don't already

00:20:54.900 --> 00:21:00.680
have some type of intake
process or something

00:21:00.680 --> 00:21:04.520
like this, where your
business stakeholders that

00:21:04.520 --> 00:21:09.350
might have ideas can
come in and request

00:21:09.350 --> 00:21:15.440
some type of company-wide,
Eli Lilly wide project

00:21:15.440 --> 00:21:21.590
that, as part of that
submission or as part

00:21:21.590 --> 00:21:23.840
of a conversation,
that your team has with

00:21:23.840 --> 00:21:29.780
them is to really try and
quantitatively determine

00:21:29.780 --> 00:21:32.810
what the benefit would be.

00:21:32.810 --> 00:21:34.880
I think that is going
to go a really far way--

00:21:34.880 --> 00:21:37.650
I'm still learning about
our whole intake process.

00:21:37.650 --> 00:21:42.030
But a lot of my
Health Net peers,

00:21:42.030 --> 00:21:44.378
now that they know that
I'm back are like, hey,

00:21:44.378 --> 00:21:45.420
can we partner with this?

00:21:45.420 --> 00:21:46.587
Can we partner it with this?

00:21:46.587 --> 00:21:48.460
And I'm like, there
is sort of a--

00:21:48.460 --> 00:21:54.330
we are trying to prioritize
the highest-ROI projects,

00:21:54.330 --> 00:21:56.730
and there is an
official intake program.

00:21:56.730 --> 00:22:01.110
So I'm pretty much just sending
them information on, hey,

00:22:01.110 --> 00:22:02.760
here's the forms.

00:22:02.760 --> 00:22:03.720
Please populate them.

00:22:03.720 --> 00:22:08.470
And yeah, that seems
to have helped a lot

00:22:08.470 --> 00:22:12.900
or helped this team scale
out their models a lot

00:22:12.900 --> 00:22:16.170
and prioritize which models to
actually put into production.

00:22:19.090 --> 00:22:21.090
I hope that answers
your question, Eric.

00:22:21.090 --> 00:22:22.368
Oh, that's very helpful.

00:22:22.368 --> 00:22:23.910
And there are
certainly pockets where

00:22:23.910 --> 00:22:26.280
we do have some of
those things in place

00:22:26.280 --> 00:22:29.910
for more general project ideas
or more general initiatives.

00:22:29.910 --> 00:22:32.760
But yeah, it's
growing, the ML case

00:22:32.760 --> 00:22:35.460
in certain parts of the
business where that just has not

00:22:35.460 --> 00:22:37.890
been implemented at all.

00:22:37.890 --> 00:22:39.390
We're trying to
find the best ways.

00:22:39.390 --> 00:22:43.170
But that's good
advice, nonetheless.

00:22:43.170 --> 00:22:44.550
I really like the--

00:22:44.550 --> 00:22:46.298
oh, you go ahead.

00:22:46.298 --> 00:22:48.840
I was just going to tell Eric
because, for those of you who

00:22:48.840 --> 00:22:51.000
don't know, he has
an awesome podcast

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:53.370
and he runs the Shiny
Developer Series,

00:22:53.370 --> 00:22:57.160
another amazing resource.

00:22:57.160 --> 00:23:01.800
I just wanted to comment
that now that I'm

00:23:01.800 --> 00:23:06.510
in this corporate team,
there's a lot that some

00:23:06.510 --> 00:23:09.330
of my old colleagues that
I'd work with closely,

00:23:09.330 --> 00:23:11.250
I wouldn't actually
recommend them

00:23:11.250 --> 00:23:14.550
to submit a use case for us.

00:23:14.550 --> 00:23:20.310
Because their problem is so
brand- or problem-specific.

00:23:20.310 --> 00:23:24.360
It's not something
that can be scaled.

00:23:24.360 --> 00:23:30.300
I notice-- I'm still
taking trainings and stuff,

00:23:30.300 --> 00:23:34.750
so excuse me for not knowing
everything about the org now.

00:23:34.750 --> 00:23:38.010
But before I left, Shiny
was becoming really popular.

00:23:38.010 --> 00:23:43.770
People loved seeing what
a Shiny app could do.

00:23:43.770 --> 00:23:50.940
I would say that I'm like
actively telling people

00:23:50.940 --> 00:23:55.500
who have no R experience but
have heavy Excel experience,

00:23:55.500 --> 00:24:00.780
for certain dashboards they
envision in Shiny that, maybe

00:24:00.780 --> 00:24:05.580
a Power BI or just Excel
dashboard is the way to go.

00:24:05.580 --> 00:24:12.000
Because they'll be able to get
what they want much faster.

00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:19.870
The overhead to design this is--

00:24:19.870 --> 00:24:23.200
making their project
a Shiny app is almost

00:24:23.200 --> 00:24:24.865
unnecessary from what I see.

00:24:27.490 --> 00:24:33.250
I love Shiny, but I think even
for finance orgs or actuaries

00:24:33.250 --> 00:24:36.790
or people that are doing
a lot of maybe heavier

00:24:36.790 --> 00:24:41.910
analysis in Excel, where
you want to convince them

00:24:41.910 --> 00:24:45.180
that, hey, Shiny might
be the best place for you

00:24:45.180 --> 00:24:48.510
is when they're trying to
get into scenario testing

00:24:48.510 --> 00:24:52.710
or machine learning
or basic things.

00:24:52.710 --> 00:24:56.460
They've got two million
records that they've somehow

00:24:56.460 --> 00:24:58.590
hodgepodged together in Excel.

00:24:58.590 --> 00:25:02.760
But when they make different
selections in an Excel

00:25:02.760 --> 00:25:05.460
dashboard, it takes three
minutes for the dashboard

00:25:05.460 --> 00:25:06.930
to re-render.

00:25:06.930 --> 00:25:09.150
That's a good use case
to put it into Shiny,

00:25:09.150 --> 00:25:10.560
because that three
minutes is now

00:25:10.560 --> 00:25:13.710
all of a sudden two seconds.

00:25:13.710 --> 00:25:17.290
That's when I'm a very big
outspoken advocate of, hey,

00:25:17.290 --> 00:25:20.640
you should have some dedicated
resources on your team,

00:25:20.640 --> 00:25:27.480
either learn about R
and Shiny or, if this

00:25:27.480 --> 00:25:30.300
is a problem that you
think could be scaled

00:25:30.300 --> 00:25:33.480
for the entire
business, then consider

00:25:33.480 --> 00:25:35.880
submitting some
type of intake form

00:25:35.880 --> 00:25:38.730
so that other business
units could also make

00:25:38.730 --> 00:25:40.200
use of this type of Shiny app.

00:25:43.590 --> 00:25:45.570
Thank you.

00:25:45.570 --> 00:25:48.590
I see a lot of great
questions coming in.

00:25:48.590 --> 00:25:53.090
And I really like the way
you describe the open culture

00:25:53.090 --> 00:25:55.950
at Centene and it being
OK to ask questions,

00:25:55.950 --> 00:26:00.260
but also providing that
time back to people

00:26:00.260 --> 00:26:02.000
and being so willing to help.

00:26:02.000 --> 00:26:06.320
I see Libby, you had asked a
question about the community

00:26:06.320 --> 00:26:08.000
at Centene, I believe.

00:26:08.000 --> 00:26:09.440
Do you want to jump in?

00:26:09.440 --> 00:26:11.100
Yeah, I would love to.

00:26:11.100 --> 00:26:13.670
So I hope I'm not
remembering incorrectly,

00:26:13.670 --> 00:26:15.710
but I'm pretty sure you
had a hand in starting

00:26:15.710 --> 00:26:19.502
R user group at Centene when
you were there previously.

00:26:19.502 --> 00:26:20.960
And so I was
wondering if you could

00:26:20.960 --> 00:26:23.120
talk a little bit about
that, because that's

00:26:23.120 --> 00:26:24.757
a difficult task.

00:26:24.757 --> 00:26:26.840
And you have to recruit
people, and getting people

00:26:26.840 --> 00:26:30.440
to meet up and help each
other and stuff like that

00:26:30.440 --> 00:26:31.500
is really challenging.

00:26:31.500 --> 00:26:32.875
So I was hoping
you could give us

00:26:32.875 --> 00:26:35.240
a little bit of a rundown
on how that happened.

00:26:35.240 --> 00:26:36.320
Yeah.

00:26:36.320 --> 00:26:37.910
Thanks, Libby, and
great to see you.

00:26:37.910 --> 00:26:45.940
So I sort of piggybacked from a
general data science community

00:26:45.940 --> 00:26:49.810
chat that we had at the company.

00:26:49.810 --> 00:26:51.370
And there were
several hundred people

00:26:51.370 --> 00:26:55.820
on this of varying backgrounds
and expertise levels.

00:26:55.820 --> 00:26:59.840
And so there was a lot of
conversation happening.

00:26:59.840 --> 00:27:03.440
There was already a Python
group that was meeting--

00:27:03.440 --> 00:27:04.970
I think every other month.

00:27:04.970 --> 00:27:09.830
So me coming in, three
weeks after I started,

00:27:09.830 --> 00:27:12.380
I got really excited
about the possibility

00:27:12.380 --> 00:27:18.690
of potentially creating
something similar for R users.

00:27:18.690 --> 00:27:23.450
And so I think it
started by just trying

00:27:23.450 --> 00:27:27.350
to figure out who owns that
already existing data science

00:27:27.350 --> 00:27:34.190
chat and see if they could help
support the idea of creating

00:27:34.190 --> 00:27:38.300
an R user group, something
to meet once a month or once

00:27:38.300 --> 00:27:40.520
every two months.

00:27:40.520 --> 00:27:42.230
Because I think at
the larger companies,

00:27:42.230 --> 00:27:48.830
getting that type of top-level,
executive stamp of approval

00:27:48.830 --> 00:27:52.460
and support can go a long way,
especially if that individual

00:27:52.460 --> 00:27:57.690
is part of the already existing
IT or data science function.

00:27:57.690 --> 00:28:01.790
And so yeah, at the time,
I created a Blogdown site.

00:28:01.790 --> 00:28:04.190
For those of you who are
familiar with R Markdown,

00:28:04.190 --> 00:28:09.260
Blogdown, it's a
package that allows

00:28:09.260 --> 00:28:17.300
you to create static websites,
static blogs with R Markdown.

00:28:17.300 --> 00:28:21.920
Distill is a very
similar concept.

00:28:21.920 --> 00:28:25.980
Quarto, now with Quarto,
you can do the same thing,

00:28:25.980 --> 00:28:28.820
creating websites.

00:28:28.820 --> 00:28:31.250
And yeah, I love the
syntax of Quarto.

00:28:31.250 --> 00:28:39.050
But anyway, I think what started
as 13 users the first month,

00:28:39.050 --> 00:28:40.970
one of them is at RStudio now.

00:28:40.970 --> 00:28:45.110
I don't know if he's on
this chat, Dave Gruenewald.

00:28:45.110 --> 00:28:46.070
He might be here.

00:28:46.070 --> 00:28:48.560
Anyway-- Hey, Dave.

00:28:48.560 --> 00:28:49.410
Hey, I'm here.

00:28:49.410 --> 00:28:49.910
Hey.

00:28:49.910 --> 00:28:51.350
Thanks, Jav.

00:28:51.350 --> 00:28:52.070
How are you?

00:28:52.070 --> 00:28:53.210
Yeah, so Dave--

00:28:53.210 --> 00:28:54.860
DAVID GRUENEWALD: Doing great.

00:28:54.860 --> 00:28:57.230
Dave taught me Shiny.

00:28:57.230 --> 00:29:01.730
I tell him this often, I owe
my current career to Dave.

00:29:01.730 --> 00:29:04.700
Because Shiny itself
made me such a better

00:29:04.700 --> 00:29:05.720
overall programmer.

00:29:05.720 --> 00:29:07.370
And the way I think
about functions

00:29:07.370 --> 00:29:10.610
and recycling code,
seriously, thank you, Dave.

00:29:10.610 --> 00:29:16.670
But yeah, what started
with about 13 users

00:29:16.670 --> 00:29:20.120
within a few months
jumped to about 100,

00:29:20.120 --> 00:29:27.950
125 monthly users that were on
this R-specific monthly meetup.

00:29:27.950 --> 00:29:29.930
So we had a really great time.

00:29:29.930 --> 00:29:32.990
The partnerships
we had with Posit,

00:29:32.990 --> 00:29:37.335
we were able to get some people
to come in and do workshops

00:29:37.335 --> 00:29:37.835
as well.

00:29:43.580 --> 00:29:45.590
Everyone was so collaborative.

00:29:45.590 --> 00:29:47.570
And it was such a
great way to see

00:29:47.570 --> 00:29:51.680
the excitement around what
you could do with all things

00:29:51.680 --> 00:29:54.470
R and even Python.

00:29:54.470 --> 00:29:59.850
Like, how can we
into these robust

00:29:59.850 --> 00:30:01.440
Python libraries or Python?

00:30:01.440 --> 00:30:04.170
And we had reticulate
sessions, where

00:30:04.170 --> 00:30:08.760
it was a co-branded
Python, R kind of workshop.

00:30:08.760 --> 00:30:13.530
We're looking at ways
in which we can actually

00:30:13.530 --> 00:30:16.740
communicate between teams
of different languages

00:30:16.740 --> 00:30:17.500
a lot easier.

00:30:17.500 --> 00:30:20.460
And so, anyway, I had a
great experience with it.

00:30:20.460 --> 00:30:23.770
That's awesome.

00:30:23.770 --> 00:30:25.250
Thank you.

