00:47:04.610 --> 00:47:09.420
I think where I've had
the most success is less

00:47:09.420 --> 00:47:13.950
so on the end-to-end consumers,
who might be looking at a Shiny

00:47:13.950 --> 00:47:17.400
app or some type
of visualization,

00:47:17.400 --> 00:47:23.520
and more on the people
generating the numbers

00:47:23.520 --> 00:47:27.357
or summaries that might later
turn into a visualization

00:47:27.357 --> 00:47:28.440
or some type of Shiny app.

00:47:28.440 --> 00:47:32.790
And so with that, where I
feel like I've had the most

00:47:32.790 --> 00:47:41.610
success with teams that are
using SAS or Excel is really

00:47:41.610 --> 00:47:44.910
with just the data-manipulation
capabilities of something

00:47:44.910 --> 00:47:47.310
like R.

00:47:47.310 --> 00:47:51.210
Firstly, the tidyverse,
which has grown so much,

00:47:51.210 --> 00:47:59.010
but the idea of a unified syntax
for data handling and just

00:47:59.010 --> 00:48:02.880
showing them the basics
of dplyr and tidyr,

00:48:02.880 --> 00:48:07.320
like, this is how you group
by one or many variables.

00:48:07.320 --> 00:48:09.960
This is how you
can do a summation.

00:48:09.960 --> 00:48:12.930
This is how you create
new columns of sums.

00:48:12.930 --> 00:48:14.520
Here's your summary data.

00:48:14.520 --> 00:48:16.620
This is how, using
tidyr, this is

00:48:16.620 --> 00:48:19.650
how I can pivot really
wide or really long.

00:48:19.650 --> 00:48:22.470
Or maybe you need to do both
for a certain calculation.

00:48:25.210 --> 00:48:28.870
Showing them the speed in
which this could be done

00:48:28.870 --> 00:48:31.270
has always really
impressed people.

00:48:31.270 --> 00:48:33.040
That's where I've
had the most success.

00:48:33.040 --> 00:48:36.700
It's kind of the basics
of these things, not even

00:48:36.700 --> 00:48:40.000
the really advanced
