00:48:59.220 --> 00:49:02.730
So it is, you went from Excel
to a lead machine learning

00:49:02.730 --> 00:49:05.730
engineer, can you tell us about
the journey, anything you found

00:49:05.730 --> 00:49:07.335
surprisingly hard or easy?

00:49:10.750 --> 00:49:13.810
Because of SQL, I had a
really good understanding

00:49:13.810 --> 00:49:17.750
of at least how tabular
data could be joined.

00:49:17.750 --> 00:49:19.720
And the different
transformations

00:49:19.720 --> 00:49:23.728
that could be done to
these data objects.

00:49:23.728 --> 00:49:25.270
I think I would have
really struggled

00:49:25.270 --> 00:49:28.270
without that basic
understanding.

00:49:28.270 --> 00:49:35.960
But having said that, I think
the parts where I really

00:49:35.960 --> 00:49:39.800
struggled at first
was function writing.

00:49:39.800 --> 00:49:43.220
Function writing was
not intuitive to me.

00:49:43.220 --> 00:49:44.910
Basic function writing was.

00:49:44.910 --> 00:49:50.900
But in general, I found
it to be very complicated.

00:49:50.900 --> 00:49:54.140
And it took a
solid, I don't know,

00:49:54.140 --> 00:49:57.380
three to six months of practice
to feel actually comfortable

00:49:57.380 --> 00:50:00.380
function writing.

00:50:00.380 --> 00:50:03.560
Even when I started
building Shiny apps--

00:50:03.560 --> 00:50:07.620
basic Shiny is quite easy.

00:50:07.620 --> 00:50:14.120
But large functions underpin
the entirety of a Shiny app.

00:50:14.120 --> 00:50:16.520
Everything you do within
Shiny is effectively

00:50:16.520 --> 00:50:18.120
writing functions.

00:50:18.120 --> 00:50:23.150
So the process of learning
Shiny and becoming

00:50:23.150 --> 00:50:26.510
more comfortable with
Shiny was very difficult

00:50:26.510 --> 00:50:30.290
and something that just
took a lot of repetitions.

00:50:30.290 --> 00:50:32.600
But it all sort of
played together.

00:50:32.600 --> 00:50:38.330
Because while Shiny, people
think of it more as a frontend

00:50:38.330 --> 00:50:43.820
type of system, it did make
me a much better programmer

00:50:43.820 --> 00:50:45.795
in the way I thought
about actual functions

00:50:45.795 --> 00:50:46.670
and function writing.

00:50:49.890 --> 00:50:55.210
Other things that I
found hard, looking back,

00:50:55.210 --> 00:50:56.950
I'm sort of embarrassed
to say this,

00:50:56.950 --> 00:51:00.310
but reproducibility
of machine learning

00:51:00.310 --> 00:51:02.440
was not something
super intuitive either.

00:51:02.440 --> 00:51:09.320
Like, being able to
reproduce a code set

00:51:09.320 --> 00:51:12.290
and get the exact same
predictions every time,

00:51:12.290 --> 00:51:15.860
I wasn't quite sure
why this wasn't working

00:51:15.860 --> 00:51:21.110
or how to create these
fixed views, setting a seed

00:51:21.110 --> 00:51:24.860
or whatever you need to do
to ensure that someone else

00:51:24.860 --> 00:51:28.490
downstream could replicate
your study or analysis and get

00:51:28.490 --> 00:51:31.990
the exact same
findings themselves.

00:51:31.990 --> 00:51:33.060
Yeah.

00:51:33.060 --> 00:51:35.120
I hope that's what you
were looking for, John.

00:51:38.660 --> 00:51:39.800
Thanks, Javier.

